{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target claims the preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible 'only if' we accept a propensity interpretation. This is historically and textually false regarding Popper's own terms. Popper explicitly defended the rationality of preferring better-corroborated theories in 'The Logic of Scientific Discovery' (1934) and 'Conjectures and Refutations' (1963) based on the methodological rule of seeking truth and the logical asymmetry between verification and falsification, decades before he fully developed his 'propensity' interpretation of probability in the 1950s and 60s. By making the propensity interpretation a necessary condition ('only if'), the target incorrectly implies that Popper's earlier defense of scientific preference was indefensible on his own terms until he adopted propensities, thereby misrepresenting the internal consistency of his philosophy which relied on critical rationalism, not probabilistic ontology, to justify theory choice.","premise":"Popper defended the preference for corroborated theories prior to and independently of his propensity interpretation of probability.","successor_question":"If the propensity interpretation is not the sole basis for Popper's defense of theory preference, what specific methodological rule in his early work justifies acting on the better-corroborated theory without invoking any form of probability?","target":"d8e19aad077566587af2a5f1022e998eae58fb81657b5797a6283b595ddd1cdc"}]}